  <h2>What goes on a fence estimate</h2>
  <p>
    A homeowner pricing a fence usually has two or three estimates side by side, and compares them
    on a handful of facts: how many feet, how tall, what material, how many gates and how the posts
    go in. Those are the job specs under the header. With them on the page, the customer can line
    your number up against the others. Without them, the cheapest estimate looks like the same
    fence.
  </p>
  <p>The line items follow the order the crew builds in:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Removal and layout:</strong> tearing out the old fence and its footings, haul-off, and stringing
      the line from the survey pins after the 811 locate.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Posts and framing:</strong> line posts, upsized gate posts, the concrete to set them, rails
      and a kickboard.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Pickets, panels and gates:</strong> pickets or panels by the foot, each gate with its hardware,
      the permit and final cleanup.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Upgrades the customer might want, such as a cap board or stain, go in the optional-items table,
    priced but outside the total.
  </p>

  <h2>How to take off a fence</h2>
  <p>
    Start from a sketch of the line with every corner, end and gate marked, then split it into
    straight runs from one terminal post to the next. A run’s sections are its length divided by
    your post spacing, rounded up, and its line posts are the sections minus one. End, corner and
    gate posts are added once each. Counting “sections plus one” on every run counts each corner
    twice.
  </p>
  <p>
    Rails are sections times rails per section. Pickets per run are the run length in inches divided
    by the picket width plus the gap, rounded up; a 1×6 is 5½ inches wide. Concrete comes from the
    hole: π × (diameter ÷ 2)² × the concrete depth, less the post, divided by 1,728 for cubic feet,
    then divided by the bag’s yield. An 80-lb bag yields about 0.60 cubic feet, but check the bag.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Hole × concrete depth</th><th>Post</th><th>Concrete</th><th>80-lb bags</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>8 in. × 24 in.</td><td>4×4</td><td>0.53 cu ft</td><td>0.9</td></tr>
      <tr><td>10 in. × 24 in.</td><td>4×4</td><td>0.92 cu ft</td><td>1.5</td></tr>
      <tr><td>10 in. × 30 in.</td><td>4×4</td><td>1.15 cu ft</td><td>1.9</td></tr>
      <tr><td>10 in. × 36 in.</td><td>4×4</td><td>1.38 cu ft</td><td>2.3</td></tr>
      <tr><td>12 in. × 36 in.</td><td>4×4</td><td>2.10 cu ft</td><td>3.5</td></tr>
      <tr><td>12 in. × 36 in.</td><td>6×6</td><td>1.73 cu ft</td><td>2.9</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Hole diameter drives concrete more than anything else: a 12-inch hole takes about half again the
    concrete of a 10-inch hole at the same depth. Depth is set by frost, soil and your local code,
    not by the template, so it is an input. The Fence Takeoff sheet runs the whole calculation for
    up to ten runs and four gates, with every spacing, width and yield editable.
  </p>

  <h2>Pricing a fence line by line</h2>
  <p>
    Price gate posts separately from line posts. They carry the gate’s weight and swing, so they are
    usually a size up and set deeper, and the price should show it. Price pickets or panels by the
    foot of fence, so a customer who shortens a run can see what it saves. Build every unit price
    from cost on the Unit Price Builder: material, labor hours at your loaded rate and consumables,
    then overhead, then profit as a
    <a href="/blog/construction-markup-vs-margin/">margin on price rather than a markup</a>. A 15%
    markup on cost is only a 13% margin.
  </p>
  <p>
    The completed example tears out an old wood fence outside Columbus, Ohio and builds 180 LF of
    6-foot cedar privacy fence on pressure-treated posts, with a 4-foot walk gate and a 10-foot
    double drive gate. Removal and layout come to $1,470, posts and framing to $4,546.60, and
    pickets, gates and the permit to $4,733. That is $10,749.60, or about $59.72 per foot of fence
    line all-in. The fence alone, without removal, gates and the permit, is about $45.39 per foot.
    There is no tax line because the contractor pays tax on materials and includes it in the prices.
    The 35% deposit is $3,762.36.
  </p>

  <h2>Property lines, 811 and rock</h2>
  <p>
    Three things on a fence job can’t be seen when you write the estimate, and each one needs a
    sentence in the terms.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The property line.</strong> The owner identifies the line and the pins, and the estimate
      says where the fence sits relative to it: 2 inches inside in the example. If nobody can find the
      pins, recommend a survey before you dig.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Utilities.</strong> Request an 811 locate before digging. It marks public lines, not the
      owner’s sprinkler pipe, pet-fence wire or landscape lighting, so the terms ask the owner to mark
      those.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rock and old footings.</strong> Price a hole that stops the auger per hole, with photos:
      $65 in the example.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Anything that changes after the estimate is signed, such as an added gate, a run moved for the
    HOA or a slope that needs stepped sections, gets written up as a
    <a href="/templates/change-order/">change order</a> and signed before the work is done. When the fence
    is finished, bill the balance on an <a href="/templates/construction-invoice/">invoice</a> that references
    the estimate number and lists the approved changes.
  </p>

  <h2>Estimate, quote or proposal?</h2>
  <p>
    For most residential fences, this customer-facing estimate is the whole sale: specs, a price,
    terms and a signature. If the customer only needs a fixed price on one page without the phase
    breakdown, a <a href="/templates/construction-quote/">construction quote</a> does that. On commercial
    fencing bid to a GC or property owner against drawings and specs, build your cost on the
    <a href="/templates/construction-estimate/">construction estimate worksheet</a> and send a
    <a href="/templates/construction-bid-proposal/">bid proposal</a> with inclusions, exclusions and alternates
    instead.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes on fence estimates</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Counting one extra post per run. Corners are shared, so “sections plus one” double-counts
      them.
    </li>
    <li>
      Pricing gate posts like line posts. They carry the gate; upsize them, set them deeper and
      price that.
    </li>
    <li>
      One concrete figure per post regardless of hole size. A 12-inch hole takes about half again
      the concrete of a 10-inch one.
    </li>
    <li>
      No property-line language. When a neighbor objects, the company that set the fence gets the
      first call.
    </li>
    <li>
      No price for rock. Without a per-hole price in writing, rock becomes a negotiation in the
      yard.
    </li>
  </ul>
